WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.730
Reviews are pouring in for Dark Phoenix, the
second cinematic attempt to adapt the classic

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.780
Marvel storyline of the same name, and they
are, for the most part, not good.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.680
The flick is currently hovering just above
20 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:13.560
What happened?

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.080
Dark Phoenix focuses on a serious problem
faced by the X-Men: how to deal with beloved

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.430
team member Jean Grey when she is imbued with
a mysterious, cosmic "Phoenix Force" which

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.300
overwhelms her to destructive results.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.270
The storyline was featured as a subplot in
2006's X-Men: The Last Stand, in case this

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:30.720
sounds strangely familiar.

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:35.620
The film is the directorial debut of longtime
X-Men franchise producer Simon Kinberg, and

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.610
judging by the reviews, it's easy to see how
the film's failure could be chalked up to

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.600
his inexperience.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.000
With a talented cast and a story that is the
stuff of legend in comics lore, Kinberg managed

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.920
to turn in a picture that Rotten Tomatoes'
Critics Consensus calls, quote, "deeply disappointing."

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.220
Writing for York Dispatch, Katie Walsh was
particularly irked by the performance of the

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.460
film's lead, Sophie Turner.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:56.360
Walsh wrote:

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.300
"Kinberg asks her to be a sledgehammer, requiring
her to shout every emotional beat and turn,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.500
relentlessly hammering the themes and messaging."

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.160
The critic also reserved plenty of scorn for
the flick's script.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.700
"What's truly amazing about Dark Phoenix is
watching the charismatic, award-winning, star-packed

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:16.920
cast flail about in this poorly written nonsense
cartoon...perhaps [its worst offense] is proving

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.140
what X-Men: Apocalypse started in its treatment
of Oscar Isaac.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.760
Despite the caliber and talent of the actor,
lackluster writing, directing, and editing

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.500
can warp a performance into something truly
dreadful.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.700
Now that's power."

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.420
That tone continues throughout the reviews,
with many viewers lamenting how curiously

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.080
flat Dark Phoenix felt, despite its emotional,
high-stakes story.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.210
William Bibbiani of The Wrap wrote:

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.840
"Turner [tries] with some success to infuse
the simplistic and irrational storyline with

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.460
some humanity, but everyone else looks like
they're more interested in what craft service

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.860
made for lunch that day...it's just a disappointingly
average superhero flick, with a familiar story,

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.720
disinterested actors, some cool action sequences,
and a whole lot of missed opportunities."

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.340
Business Insider's review also took notice
of the fact that the cast looks kind of bored

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.760
with the entire enterprise.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.780
"Uhhh, so boring."

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.360
That review stated:

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.540
"Even the characters who have been around
since X-Men: First Class can't keep things

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.040
exciting.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.080
[Jennifer] Lawrence as Mystique looks like
she'd rather be somewhere else."

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.810
Of course, not all reviewers agreed with the
consensus, although even the positive reviews

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.000
took care to point out the film's many flaws.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.120
Forbes' Mark Hughes wrote that the movie was
an acceptable, if not amazing, end to the

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:28.620
series.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:29.780
His review stated:

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.420
"While [Dark Phoenix] is not among the best
X-Men movies, it's not among the worst either.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.659
It is safely in the middle range of 'okay,'
flawed but with some things to praise and

00:02:38.659 --> 00:02:44.169
overall managing to maintain the status quo
without improving it nor tanking it...Unfortunately,

00:02:44.169 --> 00:02:47.970
Dark Phoenix continued to decline in quality
through the back half of its second act, with

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.599
choppy editing and a lot of 'why would they
do that?' or 'why wouldn't they do this instead?'

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:53.100
moments."

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.159
Most observers, however, were firmly in the
consensus camp, and more than a few were downright

00:02:58.160 --> 00:02:58.680
hostile.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.060
In perhaps the most blistering takedown leveled
at the film, Clint Worthington of The Spool

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:03.560
wrote,

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:08.540
"Dark Phoenix is a film in mourning, of its
characters, its franchise, even of its own

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:13.099
previously pristine state before delays and
reshoots turned it into a Frankenstein's monster

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:17.690
of its former self...It's a franchise that
sees its own death coming, at one point, our

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.390
heroes are captured by military personnel
who literally have the letters 'MCU' emblazoned

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.040
on their gear, and greets it with a resigned
sigh.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.700
For that reason at least, Dark Phoenix carries
a morbid fascination."

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.200
It's a good point.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.329
The movie could still leave fans excited for
the future, if not exactly for the reasons

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:35.440
the filmmakers wanted.

00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:39.859
Marvel Studios' parent company, Disney, having
recently acquired the film and television

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:44.040
assets of Fox Studios, is in an excellent
position to rehabilitate these characters

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.500
within the Marvel Cinematic Universe in the
years to come.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.580
So despite this movie's lousy reception, maybe
the future of the X-Men isn't so dark after

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:52.080
all.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:57.940
"Just because someone stumbles, loses their
way, it doesn't mean they're lost forever."

